  "notes": [
    "Curated, offline end-of-life table for the products Dzięcioł (AegisASI) identifies. It is embedded in the binary and never fetched at scan time.",
    "Branch EOL dates are copied verbatim from the endoflife.date API (https://endoflife.date/api/<product>.json) as fetched on as_of; each product cites its page. eol \"\" = branch still supported on as_of.",
    "Microsoft IIS has no lifecycle of its own: it ships with Windows Server, so each IIS version carries the extended-support end of the Windows Server release that ships it (endoflife.date/windows-server, Microsoft Lifecycle). IIS 10.0 spans Windows Server 2016-2025 and is never flagged.",
    "OpenSSH is left out of the EOL table on purpose: the project supports only its latest release and publishes no per-branch end-of-life dates. It appears only in known_vulns.",
    "Jenkins: only LTS lines (2.NNN.x) have EOL dates; weekly releases match the generic '2' line and are never flagged. WordPress: endoflife.date lists 4.7+ as still receiving security backports, so only <= 4.6 is flagged.",
    "Distribution packages (Ubuntu/Debian/RHEL) backport security fixes into old upstream branches; findings on such version strings say so instead of claiming the host is unpatched.",
    "To refresh: re-fetch the endoflife.date API for each product, update branches, bump data_version/as_of."
